  socialImage="/images/home-heating-options-australia.webp"
  socialImageAlt="A winter living room with a reverse-cycle air conditioner, an oil column heater and a folded heated throw"
>
  <p>A heater can be an easy purchase to get wrong.</p>

  <p>
    A small portable heater may warm a home office quickly but achieve very little in an open-plan
    living area. An oil column heater can provide quiet background warmth but take time to affect a
    cold room. A reverse-cycle air conditioner can heat efficiently and also cool in summer,
    although it requires suitable sizing and licensed installation.
  </p>

  <p>
    The right starting point is not the heater type. It is the room, the local climate and the way
    the space is used.
  </p>

  <p>
    This guide provides a practical overview of the main home heating options available in
    Australia. More detailed comparisons are linked throughout where a particular option deserves
    closer consideration.
  </p>

  <h2>Our Take</h2>

  <p>There is no single best heating option for every Australian home.</p>

  <p>Choose according to:</p>

  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>the size and ceiling height of the space</li>
    <li>whether it can be closed off</li>
    <li>how long it remains occupied</li>
    <li>how quickly warmth is needed</li>
    <li>acceptable noise and airflow</li>
    <li>local winter conditions</li>
    <li>existing insulation and draughts</li>
    <li>whether summer cooling is also required</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Before buying a larger heater, check whether warmth is escaping through doors, windows, open
    hallways or an inadequately insulated ceiling. Improving the room can sometimes make more
    difference than increasing heater output.
  </p>

  <h2>Start with the heating task</h2>

  <p>Different heating choices solve different problems.</p>

  <p>Ask what the household actually needs:</p>

  <h3>Quick warmth for a short period</h3>

  <p>
    A fan or ceramic heater can suit a small enclosed room used briefly, such as a home office or
    bedroom while getting dressed.
  </p>

  <h3>Quiet background warmth</h3>

  <p>
    Oil column and panel heaters provide gradual electric resistance heating without a strong fan.
    They suit smaller enclosed rooms better than large connected spaces.
  </p>

  <h3>Warmth for one person or one bed</h3>

  <p>
    An electric blanket or heated throw targets the occupant rather than heating the whole room.
  </p>

  <h3>Regular room heating and summer cooling</h3>

  <p>
    A correctly sized reverse-cycle air conditioner can provide efficient fixed heating and
    refrigerated cooling from the same system.
  </p>

  <h3>Heating several rooms</h3>

  <p>
    A ducted or multi-room system may be appropriate, but zoning, installation access and the
    building’s thermal performance become part of the decision.
  </p>

  <section aria-labelledby="heating-options-heading">
