  <h2>Fan and ceramic heaters</h2>

  <p>
    Fan-assisted heaters provide warm air quickly and are most useful in small enclosed spaces.
  </p>

  <p>
    A conventional fan heater and a ceramic heater are both generally electric resistance heaters.
    A ceramic model may include oscillation, digital controls or a more refined thermostat, but the
    word “ceramic” does not make it inherently cheaper to run.
  </p>

  <p>
    Electricity use depends mainly on rated power, operating time and thermostat cycling.
  </p>

  <p>
    Read the detailed <a href="/guides/fan-heater-vs-ceramic-heater/"
      >Fan Heater vs Ceramic Heater guide</a
    > before deciding whether the additional controls or airflow pattern are useful for the room.
  </p>

  <h2>Oil column and panel heaters</h2>

  <p>
    Oil column and panel heaters are generally quieter than fan-assisted models.
  </p>

  <p>
    An oil column heater warms sealed internal oil and its metal columns, producing a combination of
    radiant and convective warmth. It is portable but relatively heavy and slow to respond.
  </p>

  <p>
    A panel heater uses natural convection and may be wall-mounted or used with compatible feet. Its
    slim profile can preserve floor space, although furniture and curtains must not obstruct it.
  </p>

  <p>Both are electric resistance heaters. Neither design is automatically inexpensive to operate.</p>

  <p>
    The <a href="/guides/oil-column-heater-vs-panel-heater/"
      >Oil Column Heater vs Panel Heater guide</a
    > compares warm-up time, retained warmth, controls, placement and portability in more detail.
  </p>

  <h2>Radiant heaters</h2>

  <p>Radiant heaters direct warmth towards people and objects.</p>

  <p>
    They can suit short-term spot heating in a defined position, but they do not evenly warm every
    part of a room. Move outside the radiant zone and the surrounding space may still feel cold.
  </p>

  <p>
    Open or hot heating surfaces also require careful placement. Keep bedding, curtains, furniture
    and clothing outside the manufacturer’s required clearances.
  </p>

  <p>Never bring an outdoor gas heater, barbecue or other outdoor combustion appliance inside.</p>

  <h2>Electric fireplaces</h2>

  <p>An electric fireplace combines a flame-effect display with an electric heater.</p>

  <p>
    Its heating section commonly operates in a similar way to another fan or convection heater. The
    size of the fireplace surround or flame display does not indicate stronger heating performance.
  </p>

  <p>Check:</p>

  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>rated heating output</li>
    <li>fan noise</li>
    <li>electrical supply</li>
    <li>wall or recess dimensions</li>
    <li>clearances from televisions and cabinetry</li>
    <li>ventilation and servicing access</li>
    <li>whether the flame effect can operate without heat</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Treat it as supplementary heating unless its documented output and installation suit the
    complete room.
  </p>

  <h2>Electric blankets and heated throws</h2>

  <p>Electric bedding warms the bed or person rather than the room.</p>

  <p>
    An electric blanket is generally fitted to a mattress in the configuration specified by its
    manufacturer. A heated throw is designed to sit over an awake person on a chair or sofa. They
    are not interchangeable unless the instructions explicitly allow the intended use.
  </p>

  <p>
    Current Product Safety Australia guidance advises inspecting electric blankets each winter,
    warming the bed and switching the blanket off before sleeping.
  </p>

  <p>
    The <a href="/guides/electric-blankets-vs-heated-throws/"
      >Electric Blankets vs Heated Throws guide</a
    > covers fit, controls, cleaning, storage and intended use.
  </p>

  <p>
    For a broader room decision, see <a href="/guides/heating-a-bedroom-overnight-comfort/"
      >Heating a Bedroom for Overnight Comfort</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2>Reverse-cycle air conditioning</h2>

  <p>A reverse-cycle air conditioner is a heat pump.</p>

  <p>
    Rather than creating all its heat through electrical resistance, it transfers heat from the
    outdoor air into the room. This allows it to provide substantially more room heat for the
    electricity used than ordinary resistance heaters.
  </p>

  <p>It can also provide refrigerated cooling in summer.</p>

  <p>Before buying, check:</p>

  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>heating capacity</li>
    <li>the heating side of the Zoned Energy Rating Label</li>
    <li>performance for the relevant climate zone</li>
    <li>room size and connected spaces</li>
    <li>indoor airflow</li>
    <li>indoor and outdoor noise</li>
    <li>drainage</li>
    <li>electrical requirements</li>
    <li>licensed refrigerant installation</li>
    <li>filter and servicing access</li>
  </ul>

  <p>A cooling star rating does not automatically describe winter heating performance.</p>

  <p>
    Read <a href="/guides/reverse-cycle-heating-explained/">Reverse-Cycle Heating Explained</a> for
    climate performance, defrost cycles and winter operation. The broader <a
      href="/guides/air-conditioning-buying-guide/">Air Conditioning Buying Guide</a
    > covers system types and installation planning.
  </p>

  <h2>Ducted and multi-room heating</h2>

  <p>Ducted heating can distribute conditioned air through several rooms from one central system.</p>

  <p>
    Options include ducted reverse-cycle air conditioning and existing ducted gas systems.
    Multi-split air conditioning can instead connect several indoor units to one outdoor system.
  </p>

  <p>The design needs to account for:</p>

  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>room and zone loads</li>
    <li>supply and return air</li>
    <li>duct routes and insulation</li>
    <li>minimum airflow</li>
    <li>ceiling or underfloor access</li>
    <li>controls</li>
    <li>electrical capacity</li>
    <li>servicing</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Zoning can reduce heating of unused areas, but zones cannot always be closed without limit.
  </p>

  <p>
    For large connected areas, see <a href="/guides/heating-an-open-plan-living-area/"
      >Heating an Open-Plan Living Area</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2>Other fixed heating systems</h2>

  <p>
    Wood heaters, fixed gas heaters, hydronic systems and underfloor heating may suit particular
    climates, properties or renovation projects.
  </p>

  <p>They introduce site-specific considerations such as:</p>

  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>flues and ventilation</li>
    <li>fuel storage</li>
    <li>clearances</li>
    <li>local emissions or planning requirements</li>
    <li>floor and wall construction</li>
    <li>maintenance</li>
    <li>installation access</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Major fixed systems should be assessed with appropriately qualified professionals before
    equipment is ordered.
  </p>

  <p>
    Do not assume that an existing fuel type remains the most suitable choice for a replacement
    system.
  </p>

  <h2>Match the option to the room</h2>

  <h3>Small room used briefly</h3>

  <p>
    A fan or ceramic heater may provide fast supervised warmth without the cost and installation of
    a fixed system.
  </p>

  <h3>Quiet bedroom or office</h3>

  <p>
    An oil column or panel heater may provide gradual pre-bedtime or background warmth. Electric
    bedding may be more direct where the main problem is a cold bed.
  </p>

  <h3>Regularly occupied living room</h3>

  <p>
    A correctly sized reverse-cycle system may provide more efficient ongoing heating and summer
    cooling.
  </p>

  <h3>Large or open-plan space</h3>

  <p>
    Measure the complete connected area and ceiling volume. One portable heater is unlikely to
    condition a kitchen, dining room, lounge, hallway and stairwell effectively.
  </p>

  <h3>Several rooms</h3>

  <p>
    Compare ducted, multi-split or separate room systems. The right choice depends on which rooms
    are occupied at the same time and whether they can operate independently.
  </p>

  <h2>Australia does not have one heating climate</h2>

  <p>Heating requirements vary substantially across the country.</p>

  <p>
    In cooler southern, inland and elevated regions, heating performance, insulation and heat
    retention may be major household priorities.
  </p>

  <p>In temperate coastal areas, room-by-room heating may be sufficient.</p>

  <p>
    In tropical and subtropical regions, heating may be required only during occasional cool
    mornings or evenings. The heating mode of an existing reverse-cycle air conditioner or a small
    portable option may be enough.
  </p>

  <p>
    Use the climate-zone information relevant to the property rather than assuming advice written
    for another region applies unchanged.
  </p>

  <h2>Improve the room before increasing the heater</h2>

  <p>A heater cannot work efficiently against unlimited heat loss.</p>

  <p>Before increasing capacity, check:</p>

  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>gaps around doors and windows</li>
    <li>open hallways and unused rooms</li>
    <li>poorly fitted curtains or blinds</li>
    <li>large areas of cold glazing</li>
    <li>inadequate ceiling insulation</li>
    <li>blocked heater airflow</li>
    <li>furniture beside cold external walls</li>
    <li>warm air collecting near a high ceiling</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The <a href="/guides/reduce-draughts-before-buying-bigger-heater/"
      >draught-reduction guide</a
    > explains how to reduce uncontrolled air leakage without blocking required ventilation.
  </p>

  <p>
    The <a href="/guides/home-insulation-basics/">Home Insulation Basics guide</a> covers ceilings,
    walls, floors, R-values and installation quality.
  </p>

  <p>
    Where windows are the main issue, review the existing <a
      href="/guides/bedroom-lighting-and-window-coverings-what-to-compare-before-you-buy/"
      >bedroom lighting and window-coverings guide</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2>Essential portable-heater safety</h2>

  <p>Before operating a portable heater:</p>

  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>inspect the plug, cord, body and controls</li>
    <li>place it on a stable, level surface</li>
    <li>connect it directly to a suitable wall power point</li>
    <li>use one appliance per power point</li>
    <li>keep clothing, curtains, bedding and furniture at least one metre away</li>
    <li>keep it outside walkways</li>
    <li>supervise children and pets</li>
    <li>switch it off before leaving the room or going to sleep</li>
    <li>maintain working smoke alarms</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Safety features such as thermostats, timers, tip-over switches and overheat protection do not
    replace correct placement and supervision.
  </p>

  <p>Never use an outdoor gas heater, barbecue or charcoal appliance inside.</p>

  <h2>Before You Buy Checklist</h2>

  <p>Before choosing a heating option, check:</p>

  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>Which room or connected area needs heating?</li>
    <li>What are its dimensions and ceiling height?</li>
    <li>Can the space be closed off?</li>
    <li>How long will it be occupied?</li>
    <li>Is fast heat or steady background warmth required?</li>
    <li>Is the aim to warm the room, one person or the bed?</li>
    <li>Is fan noise or direct airflow acceptable?</li>
    <li>What insulation is present?</li>
    <li>Are there significant draughts?</li>
    <li>How much window glass is present?</li>
    <li>Does the option suit the local climate?</li>
    <li>What is the appliance’s rated power or heating capacity?</li>
    <li>Is summer cooling also required?</li>
    <li>Is there a safe appliance position?</li>
    <li>Can required clearances be maintained?</li>
    <li>Is there a suitable power supply?</li>
    <li>Does fixed installation require electrical, refrigerant, gas or building work?</li>
    <li>Are landlord or strata approvals required?</li>
    <li>What cleaning, servicing or fuel storage will be required?</li>
    <li>Has the product been checked against current recalls?</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Choose for the home you actually have</h2>

  <p>The largest heater is rarely the most useful starting point.</p>

  <p>
    A fan heater can suit brief use in a small room. An oil column or panel heater may suit quiet
    background heating. Electric bedding can warm a bed or person without conditioning the whole
    home. Reverse-cycle air conditioning can efficiently heat and cool a regularly occupied space.
  </p>

  <p>Start with the room, local climate and duration of use.</p>

  <p>Then consider heat loss, noise, controls, safety and installation.</p>

  <p>
    A suitable heater in a prepared room will generally provide a better result than a larger
    appliance fighting open spaces, cold windows and uncontrolled draughts.
  </p>

